      <h1>IndiaMART catalogue or your own website: which does a manufacturer need?</h1>
      <p class="article-meta">By Sutrayaan · Updated {{ page.updated_label }} · 7-minute read</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="prose">
        <p class="answer"><b>Short answer:</b> most manufacturers need both, because they do different jobs. IndiaMART brings you <b>buyers who don't know you yet</b>, and you share many of those leads with other suppliers. Your own website serves <b>buyers who already have your name</b>: from a visiting card, a referral or a quote. It checks you out, takes their drawing and sends the enquiry to you alone. Your IndiaMART catalogue page is not a substitute for a website. IndiaMART's own terms say that the web address it gives you belongs to IndiaMART, not to you.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></p>

        <h2>The difference at a glance</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>IndiaMART catalogue</th><th>Your own website</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Who owns the address</b></td><td>IndiaMART. Its terms say the URL or sub-domain it gives you is its property and "cannot be assumed to be permanent".<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></td><td>You, if the domain is registered in your company's name</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Where buyers come from</b></td><td>IndiaMART's own buyer traffic, from day one</td><td>Google, AI assistants, your visiting card, emails and referrals. It takes months to build.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Who else gets the enquiry</b></td><td>BuyLeads are shared with several relevant sellers<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup></td><td>Only you</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>What the page can show</b></td><td>IndiaMART's template, with its branding and buttons around your products</td><td>Anything: machine list, tolerances, COAs, plant photos, case studies</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>How a buyer sends a requirement</b></td><td>IndiaMART's enquiry form</td><td>Your own quote form, with drawing or spec upload</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Yearly cost</b></td><td>Free basic listing. Paid plans run from about ₹28,000–40,000 a year at entry level to several lakh at the top.<sup><a href="#s3">3</a>,<a href="#s4">4</a></sup> IndiaMART's average paying supplier pays about ₹72,000 a year.<sup><a href="#s5">5</a></sup></td><td>₹30,000–75,000 to build, then about ₹4,000–16,000 a year to run (see our <a href="/guides/factory-website-cost-india/">cost guide</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>If you stop paying</b></td><td>You lose the paid features and lead allocation. IndiaMART can also suspend or edit catalogue addresses without notice.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></td><td>The site stays yours. You only pay for domain and hosting.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h2>How IndiaMART enquiries actually work</h2>
        <p>There are two kinds, and it helps to know which one you are getting.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Direct enquiry.</b> A buyer finds your product on your catalogue page and contacts you from it.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup> These come from buyers who picked you.</li>
          <li><b>BuyLead.</b> A buyer posts a requirement on IndiaMART. IndiaMART filters it and shares it with the relevant sellers.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup> You see the requirement first. Then you spend one of your BuyLead credits to unlock the buyer's contact details.<sup><a href="#s6">6</a></sup></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Your plan decides how many BuyLeads you can unlock each week. IndiaMART's help page (updated March 2026) lists the following:<sup><a href="#s7">7</a></sup></p>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>BuyLeads per week</th><th>Daily bonus</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Mini Dynamic Catalog (monthly)</td><td>7</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mini Dynamic Catalog (annual)</td><td>10</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>TrustSEAL Pro</td><td>14</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Maximiser Pro</td><td>21</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>IM Star Pro</td><td>30</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>IM Leader Pro</td><td>40</td><td>1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>One detail matters a lot. When IndiaMART reports how many enquiries it delivered, it counts <b>each receipt of the same enquiry by multiple suppliers</b> as a separate enquiry.<sup><a href="#s8">8</a></sup> So the buyer who messaged you is often talking to your competitors too. A fast, clear reply gives you the edge.</p>

        <h2>What IndiaMART does well</h2>
        <p>To be fair, a website can't do these things on its own:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Buyers from day one.</b> IndiaMART has about 8.9 million supplier storefronts and around 2.17 lakh paying suppliers.<sup><a href="#s5">5</a></sup> Buyers come there to search. A new website has no visitors until people find it.</li>
          <li><b>Buyers who have never heard of you.</b> A trader in another state can find you through a category search.</li>
          <li><b>BuyLeads.</b> Fresh requirements arrive every week, even if no one searched for your name.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What only your own website does</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>It answers the "who are these people?" check.</b> Purchase managers and engineers look you up before they place a serious order. They expect a proper site with your machines, materials, certifications and plant photos.</li>
          <li><b>The enquiry is yours alone.</b> No one else gets a copy.</li>
          <li><b>It takes drawings.</b> A quote form with file upload lets buyers send a drawing or spec in one step.</li>
          <li><b>It shows up when people search your name.</b> Google and AI assistants like ChatGPT read your site and cite it.</li>
          <li><b>It gives you a proper email.</b> sales@yourcompany.com looks more trustworthy than a free Gmail address on a quote.</li>
          <li><b>It lasts.</b> The domain, the content and the search ranking you build stay with you, whatever happens to any platform.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Which should you put money into first?</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>What we'd suggest</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>New business, no regular buyers yet, selling a standard product</td><td>Start with IndiaMART (free or entry plan). Add a simple website within the year.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Most orders come from repeat buyers and referrals</td><td>Website first. These buyers already know your name, so they will look for you on Google, not on IndiaMART.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Custom or drawing-based work (CNC, moulds, fabrication)</td><td>Website first, with drawing upload. Big buyers check capability before they ask for a quote.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Export or large corporate buyers</td><td>Website first. Vendor registration teams usually expect a company website and a domain email address.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Paying for IndiaMART and getting steady orders</td><td>Keep it. Add a website so the buyers you win can check you out and come back directly.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h2>How to run both well</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Reply within minutes.</b> Buyers on IndiaMART often hear from several suppliers, so speed counts. Turn on app notifications.</li>
          <li><b>Put everything in one tracker.</b> Log IndiaMART and website enquiries in the same place. Then you can see which source brings orders, not just leads. Many CRMs can pull IndiaMART leads in automatically.<sup><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></li>
          <li><b>Judge cost per order, not cost per lead.</b> Divide your yearly IndiaMART fee by the orders it actually brought you.</li>
          <li><b>Keep the details the same.</b> Use the same company name, address, phone number and product names on IndiaMART, your website and your Google Business Profile.</li>
          <li><b>Give your website address to every buyer you win.</b> Put it on quotes, invoices, email signatures and visiting cards. Repeat orders then come straight to you.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>What we do</h2>
        <p>Sutrayaan builds websites for manufacturers. They are written in your industry's language and have a quote form that accepts drawings. Every enquiry goes straight into a tracker, so you can see which source turns into orders. We don't ask you to leave IndiaMART. We'd usually suggest you keep it. Prices are on the <a href="/services/">services page</a>, and you can try four <a href="/work/">live demo sites</a>.</p>

        <div class="faq" style="margin-top:12px">
          <details><summary>Is my IndiaMART catalogue page the same as a website?</summary><p>No. It is a page on IndiaMART's site, in IndiaMART's template. IndiaMART's terms say that the address belongs to IndiaMART and is not permanent. A website is on a domain your company owns, and you control what it shows.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Will my own website bring as many leads as IndiaMART?</summary><p>Not in the first months, and maybe never in raw numbers. It brings a different kind of lead: buyers who already know your name, and who aren't sending the same enquiry to ten other suppliers.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can I stop paying IndiaMART once I have a website?</summary><p>Only if IndiaMART orders are small compared with your direct business. Track both for six months in one tracker, compare cost per order, then decide.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do IndiaMART leads go to other suppliers too?</summary><p>BuyLeads do. IndiaMART filters each posted requirement and shares it with the relevant sellers. A direct enquiry is sent from your own catalogue page, but the buyer may still be talking to other suppliers.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What about TradeIndia and Justdial?</summary><p>The same logic applies. A free listing on each is worth having. Pay only where your buyers actually search, and keep your own website as the place everything points back to.</p></details>
        </div>

